(14) Battle drills associated with their current assignment. Military publications can be accessed through the Army Publishing Directorate (APD) at https://armypubs.army.mil/. Master Sergeants/First Sergeants should also continue to exploit other distributed learning programs and broaden their focus to include functional training. Senior NCOs should pursue functional courses that enhance their understanding of how the Army runs to influence and improve the Army’s systems and contribute to the success of the organization. Master Sergeants/First Sergeants should pursue opportunities to be published enhancing their written communication skills. Master Sergeants/First Sergeants should consider entering a technical program to gain nationally recognized credentials in an appropriate technical discipline. A list of technical certifications can be found on the COOL website. For information on these and other education programs, visit the Army Education Center on your installation. Master Sergeants/First Sergeants should be enrolled in and actively utilizing the Army Career Tracker (ACT) as a tool to monitor and guide career progression. The ACT in conjunction with DA PAM 600-25 will provide Soldiers and leaders the necessary information and guidance needed to maximize career progression opportunities for Military Police throughout all stages of their career. NCOs should maximize efforts to attend schools such as Air Assault, Airborne, and Pathfinder Courses given the opportunity.', 'f954439f934fb64a5089f8dde666ffc0b2c4f35938a23b034d791ffac1505184', 6, 7, null, 28, null, null, false, null),
  ('12c51892-6440-520a-94d5-1b9ce56f7791', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31Z', null, 'development', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major/Command Sergeant Major. Sergeants Major/Command Sergeants Major — recommended career management (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development by Rank', 'that used tuition assistance to the maximum extent, could possess an undergraduate degree and work towards a master’s degree in their chosen discipline. A college degree is not required for further advancement but can be a deciding factor when it comes to identifying the most qualified Sergeant Major/Command Sergeant Major. Activities such as professional reading and advanced college courses assist senior NCOs to develop and hone organizational and strategic leadership skills needed to coach, teach, and mentor leaders and Soldiers. Outstanding communications skills are required for Sergeants Major/Command Sergeants Major. Skills in community and public relations are also important since the Sergeant Major/Command Sergeant Major will often be representing the command and the Army at civic functions. Sergeants Major/Command Sergeants Major should read publications on their command team’s professional reading list as well as new and draft doctrine. Army Career Tracker (ACT) in conjunction with DA Pam 600-25 will provide Soldiers and leaders the necessary information and guidance needed to maximize career progression opportunities for Military Police throughout all stages of their career. Sergeants Major/Command Sergeants Major should also reference guidance in the 31Z section of DA PAM 600-25 as well as Command Sergeants Major branch material.', '680a0f458fe9529747d646e7ed486d1c81b431197c92578f5af237cf2b0fa987', 7, 7, null, 29, null, null, false, null),
  ('1bd855b5-b863-5722-8409-1685ab95a341', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31B', null, 'development', null, null, 'Goals for development', 'Chapter 4. MOS 31B Military Police', 'Proficiency in the three Military Police disciplines: Security and Mobility Support, Police Operations, and Detention Operations. Soldiers should continue developing interpersonal communication skills (IPC), and pursue other specialized training within the Military Police field such as: Special Reaction Team (SRT) Training, Military Police Investigator (MPI), Traffic Management and Collision Investigator (TMCI), appearing before incentive boards, exceeding Army Fitness Test (AFT) standards and basic marksman weapon standards, aggressive pursuit of further military and civilian education, and continued pursuit of duties with increased responsibilities directly related to the MOS.', '03dc6054156f73ba05cc926cd81da9b14d62746f3e2875e87c4aa245f7784250', 7, 7, null, 30, null, null, false, null),
  ('e13e3c97-5d09-5548-a98f-88f1ff4a00fc', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31B', null, 'training', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private-Specialist/Corporal — Institutional Training', 'Chapter 4. MOS 31B Military Police', 'Formal training (completion of MOS 31B course conducted under the auspices of USAMPS) is mandatory.', 'c9506cffb3f88c4778126605ec1d188d0bbd584f0a4df4a75dffd87c012e9e44', 8, 8, null, 31, null, null, false, null),
  ('a1987e12-a301-5eba-97f8-1ddd1a480307', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31B', null, 'assignments', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private-Specialist/Corporal — Operational Assignments', 'Chapter 4. MOS 31B Military Police', 'The focus during the early years of their career should be on building a strong base of technical expertise in equipment, basic MOS skills, and common Soldier tasks. Soldiers should seek responsibility and take advantage of opportunities to display their initiative, and leadership skills in team leading.', 'd4a4b9d05d2cf1b1138e20d0c8c7b1e5ac0dbb46ada7035b3f8fc44ec0b0ad78', 8, 8, null, 32, null, null, false, null),
  ('8013c5ab-e0b7-5a04-98f6-b923de4dec6e', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31B', null, 'self_development', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private-Specialist/Corporal — Self-Development', 'Chapter 4. MOS 31B Military Police', 'Skill level I Soldiers, although not mandatory, should develop and seek every opportunity to complete core undergraduate college courses. Soldiers should seek every opportunity to further develop their level of physical fitness. Soldiers should seek opportunities to separate themselves from their peers by obtaining the Expert Soldier Badge. For additional information on self- development, refer to chapter 3.', '902877d420c809990bdced7c3ca3bd9d85400bb48a3b96595142d7f7ab79d24b', 8, 8, null, 33, null, null, false, null),
  ('a546e2ca-654b-5a1f-a773-d7d51c24e978', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31B', null, 'training', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private-Specialist/Corporal — Military Training', 'Chapter 4. MOS 31B Military Police', 'Military Police Investigator (MPI), Traffic Management and Collision Investigator (TMCI), Special Reaction Team (SRT), Protective Service Training (PST), Combatives LV 1, Combatives LV 2, Domestic Violence Intervention Training (DVIT), and Child Abuse Prevention Investigative Techniques (CAPIT).', 'ac6d82c629f21b55f7e0d266c0e3243ad0bc011e83c4ad912b8580aa05e3cffd', 8, 8, null, 34, null, null, false, null),
  ('9cdd9b38-ab28-59ea-83b7-ebd5a68da620', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31B', null, 'assignments', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private-Specialist/Corporal — Critical Development', 'Chapter 4. MOS 31B Military Police', 'N/A.', '190a648f47ed454cf70ff2bd90dc842faae3f1fe19810cedf4bda2fbedb8201e', 8, 8, null, 35, null, null, false, null),
  ('2c8e013e-5aaa-5740-9391-ad7b42a05f39', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31B', null, 'assignments', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private-Specialist/Corporal — Broadening Opportunities', 'Chapter 4. MOS 31B Military Police', 'Drug Suppression Investigator, MP Investigator, Traffic Management and Collision Investigator, and PSD Specialist. Although generating force assignments is not preferred for Soldiers immediately following completion of Initial Military Training, it is important that Skill Level I Soldiers maintain law enforcement expertise and experience.', 'cee87b04dfc6a09d129ef267b73dcaecccf0bda1ac4812983eabbf9035f0e7fb', 8, 8, null, 36, null, null, false, null),
  ('22fd78e8-47fc-52e0-a7ee-5d1b686828db', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31B', null, 'training', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — Institutional training', 'Chapter 4. MOS 31B Military Police', 'BLC and EJPME I.', 'ddfec96d62e4c8b81dfe220560dcf5e578ab6d5a83acf74a5a88662e4f9dda44', 8, 8, null, 37, null, null, false, null),
  ('7d6ba86d-6ec4-5777-8107-bc83310d4fcc', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31B', null, 'assignments', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — Operational assignments', 'Chapter 4. MOS 31B Military Police', 'The focus during this phase of their career should be on developing leadership skills and tactical and technical expertise and knowledge in both operational and law enforcement assignments. The Key Leadership Position for a Sergeant is Team Leader. A Sergeant with 18 months of key leadership time may be viewed as qualified, 24 months of key leadership time may be viewed as highly qualified, and 30 months or more of key leadership time may be viewed as most qualified. Drill Sergeant may fulfill the NCO’s requirement for a key leadership position if the NCO has not had an opportunity to serve in the capacity of Team Leader in the operating force. Sergeants serving in a key leadership position higher than their current rank or position, even if it is nonconsecutive with promotion to the next higher rank and that time has been documented by a DA Form 2166-9-1 will count toward key leadership time as a Squad Leader. NCOs should continuously seek opportunities that apply their experience and leadership skills.', '8d246633c304a8a27a4dde4aec2c28fab921893660fd04e14966a61fbf6bb6a5', 8, 8, null, 38, null, null, false, null),
  ('b50ac0db-1440-57e4-967c-3e5ac809be7b', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31B', null, 'self_development', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — Self-Development', 'Chapter 4. MOS 31B Military Police', 'Sergeants should be working toward an associate’s degree. Soldiers should seek every opportunity to further develop their level of physical fitness. Soldiers should seek opportunities to separate themselves from their peers by obtaining the Expert Soldier Badge. Soldiers serving as Instructors should strive to earn their Basic Army Instructor Badge (BAIB), Senior Army Instructor Badge (SAIB), and Master Army Instructor Badge (MAIB). For additional information on self-development, refer to para 3.', '723404876be32220c8901365d5d56c575c0517f4660721458b84ec75bcf4f124', 8, 9, null, 39, null, null, false, null),
  ('1b211dd6-59c9-5227-80cf-f16286ce912b', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31B', null, 'training', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — Military Training', 'Chapter 4. MOS 31B Military Police', 'Military Police Investigation (MPI), Traffic Management and Collision Investigator (TMCI), Special Reaction Team (SRT), Protective Service Training (PST) Combatives LV 1, Combatives LV 2, Domestic Violence Intervention Training (DVIT), Child Abuse Prevention Investigative Techniques (CAPIT), Common Faculty Development - Instructor Course (CFD-IC), Non-Lethal Weapons Instructor, and Crime & Criminal Intelligence Analyst.', 'e9714300e256888b661f6e3d9399e6d2ef1391caf161aa6fd73e8c7af4cc7482', 9, 9, null, 40, null, null, false, null),
  ('d275a64e-ec13-5090-b2e1-96425c981dcd', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31B', null, 'assignments', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — Critical Development', 'Chapter 4. MOS 31B Military Police', 'Assistant Squad Leader, Plans NCO, Training Sergeant, and MMS NCO.', '66deaf3bfac416a2544e00c57210a6ed7a20fb10574364a8c9eb66fb4662c54a', 9, 9, null, 41, null, null, false, null),
  ('5d4927df-7026-5d92-899b-09499a40a15c', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31B', null, 'assignments', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — Broadening Opportunities', 'Chapter 4. MOS 31B Military Police', 'A significant portion of 31B Military Police Sergeants serve in nominative positions that require a vigorous selection process such as Drill Sergeant and Recruiter. These NCOs are selected based on their above-average performance records, aptitude scores, and demonstrated high legal, moral, and ethical standards. Those who continue to perform at an exceptional level in these assignments should be looked upon favorably for promotion potential. Additional Broadening Opportunities are MPI Investigator and PSD NCO.', '95ffbd65dfba3711bd0db0367746a0af51a10ba18a66774fa98dea9207c516be', 9, 9, null, 42, null, null, false, null),
  ('a52bb18d-e10c-56f7-8df2-b2efcca69405', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31B', null, 'training', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Institutional Training', 'Chapter 4. MOS 31B Military Police', 'ALC and EJPME I.', '3dbc8a7581cb58313c988f42490e36e0e906dcb58b4555fceba805e10edc0f81', 9, 9, null, 43, null, null, false, null),
  ('66614064-95aa-580d-b6cd-14f6a014ea73', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31B', null, 'assignments', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Operational assignments', 'Chapter 4. MOS 31B Military Police', 'The focus during this phase of their career must be on the continued development and refinement of their leadership skills and broadening their tactical and technical knowledge while serving in operational and law enforcement assignments. The Key Leadership Position for a Staff Sergeant is Squad Leader in an Operational Organization. The Key Leadership Positions for a Staff Sergeant in a Law Enforcement Activity Organization are Police Off (Det), Supv Detective, Supv Police Off (Det), Pol Off (Desk Clerk), Pol Off (Desk SGT), Police Officer, Supv Pol Off Watch Cdr, Lead Pol Off (Traffic), Pol Off (Traffic), Police Off (Traffic Inv), and Supv Pol Off (Traffic). These positions should be considered equivalent to each other as they both require technical knowledge and leadership roles and duties. Staff Sergeants should attempt to balance Law Enforcement and Combat Support assignments to increase MOS proficiency and experience. The importance of diversity between Law Enforcement and Combat Support assignments provides a well-rounded Military Police Professional to Geographic Combatant Commanders. Staff Sergeants who do not display diversity should be looked upon unfavorably for promotion potential. Individual performance and demonstrated potential in a Key Leadership Position may be the deciding factor when it comes to identifying the most qualified Staff Sergeant. A Staff Sergeant with 18 months of key leadership time may be viewed as qualified, 24 months of key leadership time may be viewed as highly qualified, and 30 months or more of key leadership time may be viewed as most qualified. Drill Sergeant may fulfill the requirement for a key leadership position if the NCO has not had an opportunity to fulfill this requirement before a centralized board. Staff Sergeants serving in a key leadership position higher than their current rank or position, even if it is nonconsecutive with promotion to the next higher rank and that time has been documented by a DA Form 2166-9-2 will count toward key leadership position time as a Sergeant First Class. Without a successful tour of key leadership time, a Staff Sergeant will not be competitive for promotion to Sergeant First Class. NCOs should continuously seek opportunities that apply their experience and leadership skills.', 'b0f5a8ffdaad4afc9932db33ac5bbd7fd0aba5ede387de5d51eaedaef6c432a7', 9, 10, null, 44, null, null, false, null),
  ('94196909-43d3-5d06-b6d6-797d59309148', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31B', null, 'self_development', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Self-Development', 'Chapter 4. MOS 31B Military Police', 'Staff Sergeants are encouraged to work toward an associate’s degree. A college degree is not required for promotion but can be a deciding factor when it comes to identifying the most qualified Staff Sergeant. Soldiers should seek every opportunity to further develop their level of physical fitness. Soldiers should seek opportunities to separate themselves from their peers by obtaining the Expert Soldier Badge. Soldiers serving as Instructors should strive to earn their Basic Army Instructor Badge (BAIB), Senior Army Instructor Badge (SAIB), and Master Army Instructor Badge (MAIB). For additional information on self-development, refer to chapter 3.', '64a6866c2f7905b232fc75afd9f1fedf92b074c82bbeea3eb2e0fc9493d2a3f9', 10, 10, null, 45, null, null, false, null),
  ('4fe02524-05fd-5909-86d2-7129c90d55b7', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31B', null, 'training', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Military Training', 'Chapter 4. MOS 31B Military Police', 'Military Police Investigator (MPI), Special Reaction Team (SRT), Conventional Physical Security/Crime Prevention, Protective Service Training (PST), Anti- Terrorism Officer (Basic), Combatives LV 1, Combatives LV 2, Combatives Master Trainer, Domestic Violence Intervention Training (DVIT), and Child Abuse Prevention Investigative Techniques (CAPIT), Common Faculty Development -Instructor Course (CFD-IC), Non-Lethal Weapons Instructor, and Crime & Criminal Intelligence Analyst, and Master Driver.', '7f378184a407882c79cd1321b927cb7dd68920264648f21e110164ba3d40bb8e', 10, 10, null, 46, null, null, false, null),
  ('28e1d068-4f9b-5f55-80b1-6f9c18cabd4e', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31B', null, 'assignments', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Critical Development', 'Chapter 4. MOS 31B Military Police', 'Staff Sergeants should strive to complete an aggregate of 24 months in Critical Development assignments, and post key leadership positions before they consider assignments not directly related to CMF 31. Critical Development positions include Operations Sergeant, PSD NCO, MP Investigator NCO, and Desk Sergeant.', '3619fb8ecb4c4fd109073cefa82b44a4a0e623b9946b48abeaae51814909eab8', 10, 10, null, 47, null, null, false, null),
  ('86e5b886-be75-5023-bcd8-ca97790bda1f', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31B', null, 'assignments', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Broadening Opportunities', 'Chapter 4. MOS 31B Military Police', 'A significant portion of 31B Military Police Staff Sergeants serve in nominative positions that require a vigorous selection process such as Drill Sergeant and Recruiter. These NCOs are selected based on their above-average performance records, aptitude scores, and demonstrated high legal, moral, and ethical standards. Those who continue to perform at an exceptional level in these assignments should be looked upon favorably for promotion potential. Drill Sergeant Leaders are selected from the very best to serve in positions vital to the Army. Those who complete Drill Sergeant Leader duty are highly qualified and should be looked upon favorably for promotion. Recruiter, Instructor/Writer, Small Group Leader, Military Science Instructor, Observer Coach/Trainer, Capabilities Division NCO (Futures Command), Training NCO, System Development Manager; and Project NCO, while career-enhancing, generally does not allow for the same level of leadership development and should not be considered appropriate alternatives to key leadership positions.', '4e5b61d448d4d8c5e5ac9c0fb17e085bfa64e619865ff734eea66f44e8c7d47b', 10, 10, null, 48, null, null, false, null),
  ('428d708f-4636-5b1a-b471-6364869c5633', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31B', null, 'training', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Institutional training', 'Chapter 4. MOS 31B Military Police', 'SLC and EJPME II.', 'b88a66ba4d2597a58f1225794a558c5305a65a18a25c95187b90151cde77e186', 10, 10, null, 49, null, null, false, null),
  ('c8c365aa-440a-5c0b-9165-8a4aac286106', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31B', null, 'assignments', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Operational assignments', 'Chapter 4. MOS 31B Military Police', 'At this point in their career, Sergeants First Class should strive for tactical and technical expertise in operational units and law enforcement assignments. The Key Leadership Position for a Sergeant First Class in an Operational Organization is Platoon Sergeant or Detachment Sergeant. The Key Leadership Positions for a Sergeant First Class in a Law Enforcement Activity Organization are LEA DET SGT, LEA Detach Sergeant, Detective (0083/MIL), Supv Detective, Supv Police Off (Det), LD Pol Off (Desk SGT), Pol Off (Desk Clerk), Pol Off (Desk SGT), Police Officer, Pol Off Watch Cdr, Spv or Supv Pol Off Watch Cdr, and Supv Pol Off (Traffic). These positions should be considered equivalent to each other as they both require technical knowledge and leadership roles and duties. Sergeants First Class should attempt to balance Law Enforcement and Combat Support assignments to increase MOS proficiency and experience. The importance of diversity between Law Enforcement and Combat Support assignments provides a well-rounded Military Police Professional to Geographic Combatant Commanders. Sergeants First Class who do not display diversity should be looked upon unfavorably for promotion potential. Individual performance and demonstrated potential in a Key Leadership Position may be the deciding factor when it comes to identifying the most qualified Sergeant First Class. A Sergeant First Class with 18 months of key leadership time may be viewed as qualified, 24 months of key leadership time may be viewed as highly qualified, and 30 months or more of key leadership time may be viewed as most qualified. Senior Drill Sergeant may fulfill the requirement for a key leadership position if the NCO has not had an opportunity to fulfill this requirement before a centralized board. Without a successful tour of key leadership position time, a Sergeant First Class will not be competitive for promotion to Master Sergeant. Sergeants First Class serving in a key leadership position higher than their current rank or position, even if it is nonconsecutive with promotion to the next higher rank and that time has been documented by a DA Form 2166- 9-2 will count toward key leadership time as a Master Sergeant. NCOs should continuously seek opportunities that apply their experience and leadership skills.', '067bc24100dc4b412771182b7349da1a8850949a2c2a5fbd5f918cd901909d0a', 10, 11, null, 50, null, null, false, null),
  ('898ccd0c-9b17-5bb1-96fd-e2b79fe1da7e', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31B', null, 'self_development', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Self-Development', 'Chapter 4. MOS 31B Military Police', 'Sergeants First Class that used tuition assistance to the max extent, could possess an associate’s degree and be working toward a bachelor’s degree. A college degree is not required for promotion but can be a deciding factor when it comes to identifying the most qualified Sergeant First Class. Soldiers should seek every opportunity to further develop their level of physical fitness. Soldiers should seek opportunities to separate themselves from their peers by obtaining the Expert Soldier Badge. Soldiers serving as Instructors should strive to earn their Basic Army Instructor Badge (BAIB), Senior Army Instructor Badge (SAIB), and Master Army Instructor Badge (MAIB). For additional information on self-development, refer to chapter 3.', '4d9aa7335e59256f90d5c03b9e9c90d138e41b65030fca71a2a547f73a731cc9', 11, 11, null, 51, null, null, false, null),
  ('bcbb14bc-9bee-58c3-a1d6-84568b0b9a31', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31B', null, 'training', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Military Training', 'Chapter 4. MOS 31B Military Police', 'MP Investigator, Battle Staff NCO, Protective Service Training, Conventional Physical Security/Crime Prevention, Anti-Terrorism Officer (Basic), Protection Integration Course, Law Enforcement Senior Leaders Course, Combatives LV 1, Combatives LV 2, Combatives Master Trainer, Domestic Violence Intervention Training (DVIT), Child Abuse Prevention Investigative Techniques (CAPIT), Common Faculty Development -Instructor Course (CFD-IC), Non-Lethal Weapons Instructor, and Crime & Criminal Intelligence Analyst, and Master Driver.', '24d6ca1a0a5bf3bed8b2d6e3d8721ad8a81f61921b86445882ae28b467e1384f', 11, 11, null, 52, null, null, false, null),
  ('8ebea448-46bd-544b-991a-371649ed7f56', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31B', null, 'assignments', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Critical Development', 'Chapter 4. MOS 31B Military Police', 'A Sergeant First Class should strive to complete an aggregate of 24 months in Critical Development assignments, and post key leadership positions before they consider assignments not directly related to CMF 31. The following assignments are Critical Development assignments: Operations NCO and PSD NCO.', '1a3f100e04b32bd353574cf96d93d664a29d425c30c34c2c33784a867cddf768', 11, 11, null, 53, null, null, false, null),
  ('2ebf736d-b8ac-522e-bc90-0071b5ec4ec6', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31B', null, 'assignments', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Broadening Opportunities', 'Chapter 4. MOS 31B Military Police', 'A portion of 31B Military Police Sergeants First Class serve as Senior Drill Sergeants, a position that requires a rigorous selection process. These NCOs are selected based on their above-average performance records, aptitude scores, and demonstrated high legal, moral, and ethical standards. Those who continue to perform at an exceptional level in these assignments should be looked upon favorably for promotion potential. Drill Sergeant Leaders are selected from the very best to serve in positions vital to the Army. Those who complete Drill Sergeant Leader duty are highly qualified and should be looked upon favorably for promotion. Recruiter, Instructor/Writer, Small Group Leader, BOLC Instructor, Military Science Instructor, Observer Coach/ Trainer, Capabilities Division NCO (Futures Command), Talent Mgmt NCO, Career MGMT NCO, Tactical NCO (BOLC/CCC), System Development NCO, Training Developer, Senior Training Management NCO, and Capability Developer; while career- enhancing, generally does not allow for the same level of leadership development and should not be considered appropriate alternatives to key leadership positions.', '30e406b9900feb7ec8052397cb4c5be7bbd142c7053b5b893d0b817c12c49b34', 11, 12, null, 54, null, null, false, null),
  ('c3e3ca02-d524-59aa-9576-1722236e283a', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31B', null, 'training', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/ First Sergeant — Institutional training', 'Chapter 4. MOS 31B Military Police', 'MLC, SMC, and EJPME II.', 'fe0089d131112e214cf6a8c85d01ec7f9daaf30630662db82641557af093d373', 12, 12, null, 55, null, null, false, null),
  ('9ae92693-6fca-55c0-969e-c396429398a9', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31B', null, 'assignments', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/ First Sergeant — Operational assignments', 'Chapter 4. MOS 31B Military Police', 'At this level, Master Sergeants should demonstrate tactical and technical mastery of the required knowledge, skills and behaviors in both operational and law enforcement assignments. The Key Leadership Position for a Master Sergeant is First Sergeant. Individual performance and demonstrated potential as a First Sergeant may be the deciding factor when it comes to identifying the most qualified Master Sergeant. A Master Sergeant with 18 months of key leadership time may be viewed as qualified, 24 months of key leadership time may be viewed as highly qualified, and 30 months or more of key leadership time may be viewed as most qualified. Without a successful tour of key leadership time as a First Sergeant, a Master Sergeant will not be competitive for promotion to Sergeant Major. Master Sergeants serving in a key leadership position higher than their current rank or position, even if it is nonconsecutive with promotion to the next higher rank and that time has been documented by a DA Form 2166-9-2 will count toward key leadership time as an Operations Sergeant Major or Command Sergeant Major. NCOs should continuously seek opportunities that develop and refine their experience and leadership skills.', 'ff6f13ba5eabbc9e959141eec578bdf59b57e69afe43a063db76b6c372438f2b', 12, 12, null, 56, null, null, false, null),
  ('8e8ebf4b-8c6e-5bbe-9038-ba8720876d26', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31B', null, 'self_development', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/ First Sergeant — Self-Development', 'Chapter 4. MOS 31B Military Police', 'Master Sergeants should be working towards a bachelor’s degree. A college degree is not required for promotion but can be a deciding factor when it comes to identifying the most qualified Master Sergeant. Soldiers should seek every opportunity to further develop their level of physical fitness. Soldiers should seek opportunities to separate themselves from their peers by obtaining the Expert Soldier Badge. Soldiers serving as Instructors should strive to earn their Basic Army Instructor Badge (BAIB), Senior Army Instructor Badge (SAIB), and Master Army Instructor Badge (MAIB). For additional information on self-development, refer to chapter 3.', '578d5bb1accaf43b0c8a9c70ef02e057b5d524610f13225dbdd49f23ba585167', 12, 12, null, 57, null, null, false, null),
  ('ea537f3a-3bdb-5fdd-acfe-116920926a40', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31B', null, 'training', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/ First Sergeant — Military Training', 'Chapter 4. MOS 31B Military Police', 'Battle Staff NCO, Protection Integration Course, Combatives Master Trainer, Common Faculty Development -Instructor Course (CFD-IC), Non-Lethal Weapons Instructor, and Crime & Criminal Intelligence Analyst, Master Driver, and Law Enforcement Senior Leaders Course.', '49ef814fb8ccac1a4ba93172333c6d40141e880e52a0de6f1aac0a3c7094bc26', 12, 12, null, 58, null, null, false, null),
  ('cab6423e-a4b5-55fb-b179-8dca60726a85', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31B', null, 'assignments', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/ First Sergeant — Critical Development', 'Chapter 4. MOS 31B Military Police', 'A Master Sergeant should strive to complete an aggregate of 24 months in Critical Development assignments, and post key leadership positions before they consider assignments not directly related to CMF 31. The following assignments are Critical Development assignments: PM Operations NCO, Operations NCO, and Provost NCO.', '04eabf30f38de207c9023dc1db37d4d2f3b316897ebefeb74c5329ca1b3bab47', 12, 12, null, 59, null, null, false, null),
  ('cc983526-10e8-5ee7-be68-8535b9ecaddc', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31B', null, 'assignments', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/ First Sergeant — Broadening Opportunities', 'Chapter 4. MOS 31B Military Police', 'Inspector General NCO, EO Advisor, Senior PDNCO, White House Fellowship, Protection Cell NCO, and PSD NCO.', '3b0b37bf50816dc8084ee5b401980840a7c0650da470e22397d505fd65589571', 12, 12, null, 60, null, null, false, null),
  ('6aeca62e-1031-5776-82ed-85fc1fb2acc6', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31D', null, 'development', null, null, 'Overview', 'Chapter 5. MOS 31B Professional Development Model', 'The purpose of the Military Police Professional Development Model is to advise Soldiers and NCOs on career opportunities to pursue professional and personal goals while meeting the needs of the Army. Access to CMF 31 Career Maps can be accessed by visiting the Army Career Tracker (ACT) homepage (https://actnow.army.mil) or the DA PAM 600-25 Smartbook at https://www.milsuite.mil/book/groups/smartbook-da-pam-600- 25.', '4310f25abfcf90581f16a295d901ee400ce38bd94f6e8c54c93dc4252afdd765', 12, 13, null, 61, null, null, false, null),
  ('7b99e6e2-3f5f-5818-884b-ef43aefa4a9b', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31D', null, 'development', null, null, 'Goals for development', 'Chapter 5. MOS 31B Professional Development Model', 'CID Special Agents are proficient in criminal law and procedure, criminalistics, crime scene processing, testimonial evidence, investigations of crimes against persons and property, physical evidence, drug investigation, fraud and waste, investigative reports, special investigative techniques, criminal and police intelligence, and protective services. CID Special Agents may apply for and attend training to become qualified in advanced specialized fields, areas or skills such as digital forensic collectors/examiners or as forensic science technicians. CID Special Agents may possess technical certifications through the International Association for Identification (IAI), the American Board of Medico-Legal Death Investigators (ABMDI), or the American Academy of Forensics Science (AAFS). CID Special Agents may train at or with the FBI National Academy, Federal Law Enforcement Training Center, Canadian Police College, and Metropolitan Police Academy. Aggressive pursuit of further military and civilian education, continued pursue of duties with increased responsibilities directly related to MOS.', '711e875672f7c82a410cf827633fc3a5ac0a74b6694d1088d2028b0b2dcd1f81', 13, 13, null, 62, null, null, false, null),
  ('b76fee69-eb6e-5fa9-993f-6c9a9ef9d9a3', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31D', null, 'training', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — Institutional Training', 'Chapter 5. MOS 31B Professional Development Model', 'BLC and EJPME I. Completion of CID Special Agent Course (CIDSAC) conducted under the auspices of USAMPS is mandatory.', 'eabd2978007d3f9e97e1d3fd19f2e262dbf62eeba6d0a1d88eddd4c02ded4407', 13, 13, null, 63, null, null, false, null),
  ('72b0cb97-8628-5493-a544-14c35a6ea71a', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31D', null, 'assignments', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — Operational Assignments', 'Chapter 5. MOS 31B Professional Development Model', 'The focus during this phase of their career should be on developing leadership skills and tactical and technical expertise and knowledge in both operational and law enforcement assignments. The Key Leadership Position for a Sergeant is Special Agent. A Sergeant with 18 months of key leadership time may be viewed as qualified, 24 months of key leadership time may be viewed as highly qualified, and 30 months or more of key leadership time may be viewed as most qualified. Sergeants serving in a key leadership position higher than their current rank or position, even if it is nonconsecutive with portion to the next higher rank and that time has been documented by a DA Form 2166-9-1 will count toward key leadership time as a Special Agent. NCOs should continuously seek opportunities that applies their experience and leadership skills. Sergeants should broaden technical, tactical, and leadership skills in support of CID combat and peacetime missions by honing their investigative competencies and technical skills. Sergeants should maintain their position of CID Special Agent a minimum of 24 months before moving to other assignments or seeking to obtain specialized advanced skills such as Digital Forensic Science (FSE) Technician.', '7f3e6fcb7d7b93d4b41fd2429dbed1bccfdf3977f4822613df2ac87463d109b0', 13, 14, null, 64, null, null, false, null),
  ('38d0362a-53bd-5a21-92a4-1d82f5929fa3', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31D', null, 'self_development', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — Self-Development', 'Chapter 5. MOS 31B Professional Development Model', 'Refer to the applicable chapters of the Soldier’s Manual, MOS 31D, CID Special Agent SL2 for Sergeants’ tasks. Sergeants should possess an associate’s degree and be working toward a bachelor’s associate’s degree. Soldiers should seek every opportunity to further develop their level of physical fitness. Soldiers should seek opportunities to separate themselves from their peers by obtaining the Expert Soldier Badge. For additional information on self-development, refer to chapter 3.', 'ccaffff13c3a7b2090a4e0aeb50a918bf3355a9ba911cb2ffc347b97b90cb349', 14, 14, null, 65, null, null, false, null),
  ('f6afa6f5-1432-5044-86b6-32d5e8c57e34', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31D', null, 'training', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — Military Training', 'Chapter 5. MOS 31B Professional Development Model', 'Common Faculty Development-Instructor Course (CFD-IC), Protective Services Training, Child Abuse Prevention Investigative Techniques, Domestic Violence Intervention Training, Special Victim Capability, and Advanced Crime Scene Investigative Techniques.', 'd8a0b25c7c8b6165681e7fd91015ff67898c80cc1d67bf6440f6eb6e2981542a', 14, 14, null, 66, null, null, false, null),
  ('e5ed5365-2250-53d5-b0f5-3492300b757b', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31D', null, 'assignments', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — Critical Development', 'Chapter 5. MOS 31B Professional Development Model', 'A Sergeant should strive to complete an aggregate of 24 to 36 months as a CID Case Agent to develop knowledge, skills, and attributes to prepare for future assignments.', 'ad1c232e6d1cfc9727a3492cbbc48dd49259bf9ffb5b809f18d1127577964478', 14, 14, null, 67, null, null, false, null),
  ('e65599de-6ce3-5648-84ca-832c22c9de65', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31D', null, 'assignments', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — Broadening Opportunities', 'Chapter 5. MOS 31B Professional Development Model', 'CID Special Agents are not authorized in nominative assignments such as DA Select Recruiter, Drill Sergeant, and Observer Coach/Trainer. CID Special Agents are also not authorized in non-nominative assignments such as ROTC Military Science Instructors. CID Agents should continue to seek opportunities related to CMF 31 to enhance technical and tactical competences.', 'd9c9368cc1bc91735ae22438e24aecb57702f8b68f2ce7631f9b4fd2ec863357', 14, 14, null, 68, null, null, false, null),
  ('b4996f9f-1137-564a-a011-181f74de9f76', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31D', null, 'training', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Institutional Training', 'Chapter 5. MOS 31B Professional Development Model', 'ALC and EJPME I.', '3dbc8a7581cb58313c988f42490e36e0e906dcb58b4555fceba805e10edc0f81', 14, 14, null, 69, null, null, false, null),
  ('16a80dad-456d-5ed9-bee5-ac578cbd5b1d', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31D', null, 'assignments', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Operational Assignments', 'Chapter 5. MOS 31B Professional Development Model', 'Key Leadership Position for a Staff Sergeant is Special Agent. The focus during this phase of their career must be on continued development and refinement of their leadership skills and while serving in operational assignments. The Key Leadership Position for a Staff Sergeant is Special Agent. Individual performance and demonstrated potential as a Special Agent may be the deciding factor when it comes to identifying the most qualified Staff Sergeant. A Staff Sergeant with 18 months of key leadership time may be viewed as qualified, 24 months of key leadership time may be viewed as highly qualified, and 30 months or more of key leadership time may be viewed as most qualified. Staff Sergeants serving in a key leadership position higher than their current rank or position, even if it is nonconsecutive with promotion to the next higher rank and that time has been documented by a DA Form 2166-9-2 will count toward key leadership time as a Detachment Sergeant. CID Special Agent Staff Sergeants will continue to serve primarily as case agents conducting investigations. Staff Sergeant should maintain these positions for a minimum of 18 months. If possible, Staff Sergeant CID Special Agents will be assigned to positions where they will serve as Evidence Custodians, Criminal Intelligence NCO, Digital Forensic Examiner (DFE), Protective Service Agent or Forensic Science Technician (FST) when they do not already possess that experience. Without a successful tour of key leadership time related to CMF 31 as a Special Agent, a Staff Sergeant will not be competitive for promotion to Sergeant First Class. NCOs should continuously seek opportunities that applies their experience and leadership skills.', '23af1d3f88e92879c53dd35841184419560b4fa123449833f11a993df6071087', 14, 15, null, 70, null, null, false, null),
  ('08798c5d-8aa2-5a57-a883-15adaf878916', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31D', null, 'self_development', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Self-Development', 'Chapter 5. MOS 31B Professional Development Model', 'Refer to the applicable chapters of the Soldier’s Manual, MOS 31D, CID Special Agent SL3 for Staff Sergeants’ tasks. Staff Sergeants should continue the link between personal self-development activities and military career goals by using the professional development model. Staff Sergeants should possess an associate’s degree and be working toward a bachelor’s degree. Soldiers should seek every opportunity to further develop their level of physical fitness. Soldiers should seek opportunities to separate themselves from their peers by obtaining the Expert Soldier Badge. Soldiers serving as Instructors should strive to earn their Basic Army Instructor Badge (BAIB), Senior Army Instructor Badge (SAIB), and Master Army Instructor Badge (MAIB). For additional information on self-development, refer to chapter 3.', '9d1db8958f9edfa7ff1b9b74c557b595a847c6903db8eb5e0d702e10216a64ac', 15, 15, null, 71, null, null, false, null),
  ('1d83f8e1-5b19-58c6-9fb4-408a1ccecddf', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31D', null, 'training', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Military Training', 'Chapter 5. MOS 31B Professional Development Model', 'Protective Services Training, Common Faculty Development-Instructor Course (CFD-IC) Child Abuse Prevention Investigative Techniques, Domestic Violence Intervention Training, Special Victim Capability, Advanced Crime Scene Investigative Techniques, and Crime and Criminal Intelligence Analyst Course.', '1adc025450fe8d06a1fc413a5736ecffca95eaa248edba9598de328b8d752d58', 15, 15, null, 72, null, null, false, null),
  ('c2f47182-f142-5c47-8798-9ecfc0fe6457', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31D', null, 'assignments', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Critical Development', 'Chapter 5. MOS 31B Professional Development Model', 'Protective Service Agent and Criminal Intelligence NCO.', 'd04a3bdd69b295f943e9b9be43593737b1a6890a12ee910dbcd65fd2e3af6080', 15, 15, null, 73, null, null, false, null),
  ('8aff415c-c794-5042-baa0-c6ad46fbc7e3', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31D', null, 'assignments', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Broadening Opportunities', 'Chapter 5. MOS 31B Professional Development Model', 'Instructor/Writer and Small Group Leader. CID Special Agents are not authorized in nominative assignments such as DA Select Recruiter, Drill Sergeant, and Observer Coach/Trainer. CID Agents should continue to seek opportunities related to CMF 31 to enhance technical and tactical competencies.', '0efd84208b30700de9853a3c63d97c54914b6d6a0a5c99552b201f872b4f95f4', 15, 15, null, 74, null, null, false, null),
  ('73f94910-5e3b-5309-9a7c-a0a4758c299f', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31D', null, 'training', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Institutional Training', 'Chapter 5. MOS 31B Professional Development Model', 'SLC and EJPME II.', 'b88a66ba4d2597a58f1225794a558c5305a65a18a25c95187b90151cde77e186', 15, 15, null, 75, null, null, false, null),
  ('28cdad9e-189c-57bc-85bb-0216277cbc74', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31D', null, 'assignments', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Operational Assignments', 'Chapter 5. MOS 31B Professional Development Model', 'At this point of their career, Sergeants First Class should strive for tactical and technical expertise in operational units and law enforcement assignments. The Key Leadership Position for a Sergeant First Class is Detachment Sergeant. Individual Performance and demonstrated potential as a Detachment Sergeant may be the deciding factor when it comes to identifying the most qualified Sergeant First Class, a Sergeant First Class with 18 months of key leadership time may be viewed as qualified, 24 months of key leadership time may be viewed as highly qualified, and 30 months or more of key leadership time may be viewed as most qualified. Without a successful tour as a Detachment Sergeant, a Sergeant First Class will not be competitive for promotion to Master Sergeant. After serving as a Detachment Sergeant, Sergeants First Class should seek additional time as a Detachment Sergeant or in a broadening assignment. Sergeants First Class serving in a key leadership position higher than their current rank or position, even if it is nonconsecutive with promotion to the next higher rank and that time has been documented by a DA Form 2166-9-2 will count toward key leadership time as a First Sergeant.', '13eaf884c42873e5c52db5d02e4ddf55490f27954d503fc45682cbcf76c00cd3', 15, 15, null, 76, null, null, false, null),
  ('22695c95-1103-5718-96f8-f1be5d9ebbd0', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31D', null, 'self_development', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Self-Development', 'Chapter 5. MOS 31B Professional Development Model', 'Refer to the applicable chapters of the Soldier’s Manual, MOS 31D, CID special agent SL4 for Sergeants First Class. Sergeants First Class should continue the link between personal self-development activities and military career goals by using the professional development model. Sergeants First Class should possess an associate’s degree and be working toward a bachelor’s degree. Soldiers should seek every opportunity to further develop their level of physical fitness. Soldiers should seek opportunities to separate themselves from their peers by obtaining the Expert Soldier Badge. Soldiers serving as Instructors should strive to earn their Basic Army Instructor Badge (BAIB), Senior Army Instructor Badge (SAIB), and Master Army Instructor Badge (MAIB). For additional information on self-development, refer to chapter 3.', '6cf29e7b12b57a0da58b5489ae218f0cfb5accd7e9836ff89522a3848b8caec9', 15, 16, null, 77, null, null, false, null),
  ('12a9aeb9-3747-5082-a007-edf501f706bb', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31D', null, 'training', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Military Training', 'Chapter 5. MOS 31B Professional Development Model', 'Advanced Crime Scene Investigative Techniques and Crime and Criminal Intelligence Analyst Course.', '2b5d56cec38dbaa8d9bb0f0fab7031b20617b364d948094483ffffd591fb5f78', 16, 16, null, 78, null, null, false, null),
  ('4e6bb385-1412-5c80-a12c-9a6c931ff050', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31D', null, 'assignments', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Critical Development', 'Chapter 5. MOS 31B Professional Development Model', 'A Sergeant First Class should strive to complete an aggregate of 24 months in Key Development assignments. The following assignments are Critical Development assignments Operations NCO, Evidence Custodian, Criminal Intelligence NCO and Protective Service NCO. Evidence Custodian, Protective Service NCO, Operations NCO, and Criminal Intel NCO.', '94b5eb94df104b4477b4015d7253fc9694e2ceb9165331f5121a1912e47e4f02', 16, 16, null, 79, null, null, false, null),
  ('a5091a2a-b63c-5ce4-8935-1fcf2136e7f6', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31D', null, 'assignments', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Broadening Opportunities', 'Chapter 5. MOS 31B Professional Development Model', 'Instructor/Writer, Small Group Leader, Training Developer NCO, Talent Management NCO (HRC), IG NCO, and System Development NCO Talent Management NCO, Training Developer, System Development NCO, and Inspector General NCO. CID Special Agents are not authorized in nominative assignments such as DA Select Recruiter, Drill Sergeant, and Observer Coach/Trainer. CID Special Agents are also not authorized in non- nominative assignments such as ROTC Military Science Instructors. CID Agents should continue to seek opportunities related to CMF 31 to enhance technical and tactical competences competencies.', 'f4301c74523f022b9387bd8ebcc7305922b1472a5c283b1cd78d4b870d197afc', 16, 16, null, 80, null, null, false, null),
  ('e59f9932-a29d-5ff7-a36f-836049ab59a2', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31D', null, 'training', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/First Sergeant — Institutional Training', 'Chapter 5. MOS 31B Professional Development Model', 'MLC, SMC, and EJPME II.', 'fe0089d131112e214cf6a8c85d01ec7f9daaf30630662db82641557af093d373', 16, 16, null, 81, null, null, false, null),
  ('ff2a6339-50c3-535c-96eb-cf8042e84837', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31D', null, 'assignments', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/First Sergeant — Operational Assignments', 'Chapter 5. MOS 31B Professional Development Model', 'At this level, Master Sergeants should demonstrate tactical and technical mastery of the required knowledge, skills and behaviors in both operational and law enforcement assignments. The Key Leadership Position for a Master Sergeant is First Sergeant. Individual Performance and demonstrated potential as a First Sergeant may be the deciding factor when it comes to identifying the most qualified Master Sergeant, a Master Sergeant with 18 months of key leadership time may be viewed as qualified, 24 months of key leadership time may be viewed as highly qualified, and 30 months or more of key leadership time may be viewed as most qualified. Without a successful tour as a First Sergeant, a Master Sergeant will not be competitive for promotion to Sergeant Major. After serving as a First Sergeant, Master Sergeants should seek additional First Sergeant time by serving as the First Sergeant of a Headquarters Company or in a broadening assignment. Master Sergeants serving in a key leadership position higher than their current rank or position, even if it is nonconsecutive with promotion to the next higher rank and that time has been documented by a DA Form 2166-9-3 will count toward key leadership time as an Operations Sergeant Major or', 'f7d6beec5f34e19f3923a49a296978905252c703217dd9d93ae21e7d0a396c2f', 16, 16, null, 82, null, null, false, null),
  ('be7448e9-da0d-53b1-9e03-949c46b56ca0', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31D', null, 'development', 'E-9', null, 'Command Sergeant Major — overview', 'Chapter 5. MOS 31B Professional Development Model', '(c) Self-Development. In addition, NCOs should continue to pursue individual professional development activities to include civilian education and the Career Development Model and reading program using the recommended reading lists for their appropriate SL and MOS. Master Sergeants should possess an associate’s degree and be working toward a bachelor’s degree. Soldiers should seek every opportunity to further develop their level of physical fitness. Soldiers should seek opportunities to separate themselves from their peers by obtaining the Expert Soldier Badge. Soldiers serving as Instructors should strive to earn their Basic Army Instructor Badge (BAIB), Senior Army Instructor Badge (SAIB), and Master Army Instructor Badge (MAIB). For additional information on self-development, refer to chapter 3.
(d) Military Training. Law Enforcement Senior Leaders Course and Military Police Pre- Command Course.
(e) Critical Development. A Master Sergeant should strive to complete an aggregate of 24 months in Key Development assignments before they consider assignments not directly related to CMF 31. The following assignments are Critical Development assignments - Detachment Sergeant and Operations NCO.
(f) Broadening Opportunities. Assistant Inspector General, EO Advisor, and CID Enlisted Development NCO. CID Agents should continue to seek opportunities related to CMF 31 to enhance technical and tactical competencies.', '1720c22cd81819d7844e35fc469665c63f881a66858f7a05f10c5e0d09d5eaea', 16, 17, null, 83, null, null, false, null),
  ('be3e56be-0435-5ce9-a4e1-a1fbf5e22f9d', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31E', null, 'development', null, null, 'Goals for development', 'Chapter 6. MOS 31E Corrections/Detention (C/D) Specialist', 'Proficiency in C/D Operations include: reviewing and implementing emergency action plans that address minor/major prisoner disturbances, prisoner escapes, and mass casualty events associated with natural disasters, providing supervision of custody/control and accountability of US military prisoners or other detained persons (during time of war/conflict) population. Soldiers should continue developing IPC skills, pursuing other specialized training in the C/D field, participating as a Special Reaction Team (SRT) member, appearing before incentive boards, exceeding Army Fitness Test (AFT) and basic marksmanship qualification standards, aggressive pursuit of further military and civilian education, and pursuit of duties with increased responsibilities directly related to MOS and American Correctional Association.', '7c0c7bf8d887b0f949304d8491d8d40957f317306347c7043f5441b1afefa7c9', 17, 17, null, 84, null, null, false, null),
  ('d2a7e110-7880-5941-999b-4eef01fac09d', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31E', null, 'training', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private–Specialist/Corporal — Institutional Training', 'Chapter 6. MOS 31E Corrections/Detention (C/D) Specialist', 'Formal training (completion of MOS 31E course conducted under the auspices of USAMPS) is mandatory.', '4f134e1077f87cd90f5504614862d92c8508fed816e9ef00dac8d5786d35acd1', 17, 17, null, 85, null, null, false, null),
  ('aaf4b901-e872-5347-9906-f9f9aeef50e7', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31E', null, 'assignments', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private–Specialist/Corporal — Operational Assignments', 'Chapter 6. MOS 31E Corrections/Detention (C/D) Specialist', 'The focus during the early years of their career should be on building a strong base of technical expertise in equipment accountability, basic MOS skills, and common Soldier tasks. Key development can be acquired by serving as a C/D Specialist and MP Investigator. Soldiers should seek responsibility and take advantage of opportunities to display their leadership skills, initiative, and motivation.', '6b76b536b403c02311ad630ea1353a783b90dd9f9e4470cfaed96b26dc075c11', 18, 18, null, 86, null, null, false, null),
  ('cdeba969-23fb-5d11-919a-d28b6f6c3b45', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31E', null, 'self_development', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private–Specialist/Corporal — Self-Development', 'Chapter 6. MOS 31E Corrections/Detention (C/D) Specialist', 'Skill level I Soldiers should develop and seek every opportunity to complete core undergraduate college courses and the American Corrections Association Certified Corrections Officer Course. Soldiers should seek every opportunity to further develop their level of physical fitness. Soldiers should seek opportunities to separate themselves from their peers by obtaining the Expert Soldier Badge. For additional information on self- development, refer to chapter 3.', '8244efb7bcfb9cb61de56c3e0cd9979852ea053319c76505bbdb2082afc7c731', 18, 18, null, 87, null, null, false, null),
  ('dc4a5a9d-6d4b-5d21-af88-393b5ee500d6', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31E', null, 'training', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private–Specialist/Corporal — Military Training', 'Chapter 6. MOS 31E Corrections/Detention (C/D) Specialist', 'MP Investigator, Special Reaction Team, Combatives LV 1, and Combatives LV 2.', '7ca30268348410acca94382fc1463a6c637953ed8a748fb4e626f7998dc0c272', 18, 18, null, 88, null, null, false, null),
  ('48da7e1b-dd21-5d12-a829-cb4a8fa5584c', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31E', null, 'assignments', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private–Specialist/Corporal — Critical Development', 'Chapter 6. MOS 31E Corrections/Detention (C/D) Specialist', 'N/A.', '190a648f47ed454cf70ff2bd90dc842faae3f1fe19810cedf4bda2fbedb8201e', 18, 18, null, 89, null, null, false, null),
  ('8992f8f0-1a69-500c-b4d4-facc42fb814e', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31E', null, 'assignments', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private–Specialist/Corporal — Broadening Opportunities', 'Chapter 6. MOS 31E Corrections/Detention (C/D) Specialist', 'MP Investigator.', 'b0bc59d4f71e896eb3abda74ecac84e19a690d722bfafdf97bff1a6b2a343775', 18, 18, null, 90, null, null, false, null),
  ('3227771e-3531-5cd6-bd29-fc0fbd14f1e0', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31E', null, 'training', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — Institutional Training', 'Chapter 6. MOS 31E Corrections/Detention (C/D) Specialist', 'BLC and EJPME I.', 'ddfec96d62e4c8b81dfe220560dcf5e578ab6d5a83acf74a5a88662e4f9dda44', 18, 18, null, 91, null, null, false, null),
  ('089bb9b1-fa54-59f0-a580-ac9f4a877434', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31E', null, 'assignments', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — Operational Assignments', 'Chapter 6. MOS 31E Corrections/Detention (C/D) Specialist', 'The focus during this phase of their career should be on developing leadership skills and tactical and technical expertise and knowledge in both operational and corrections assignments. The Key Leadership Position for a Sergeant is Team Leader. A Sergeant with 18 months of key leadership time may be viewed as qualified, 24 months of key leadership time may be viewed as highly qualified, and 30 months or more of key leadership time may be viewed as most qualified. Sergeants serving in a key leadership position higher than their current rank or position, even if it is nonconsecutive with promotion to the next higher rank and that time has been documented by a DA Form 2166-9- 1 will count toward key leadership time as a Squad Leader. NCOs should continuously seek opportunities that apply their experience and leadership skills.', 'de5f79e2d51bf43652cf158b43d5d3ade59e590b95935e9c91cc8c47f6090cce', 18, 18, null, 92, null, null, false, null),
  ('7a27fd83-5e38-50a2-9191-723125eba160', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31E', null, 'self_development', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — Self-Development', 'Chapter 6. MOS 31E Corrections/Detention (C/D) Specialist', 'American Corrections Association Certified Corrections Officer Course. Sergeants are encouraged to work toward an associate’s degree. Soldiers should seek every opportunity to further develop their level of physical fitness. Soldiers should seek opportunities to separate themselves from their peers by obtaining the Expert Soldier Badge. For additional information on self-development, refer to chapter 3.', 'cde31c3ddffee8fce0dd255a013388e159f28f7896e417be752cb392d7b789e2', 18, 18, null, 93, null, null, false, null),
  ('3f4eb274-c891-5606-ad85-838eb61a9dec', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31E', null, 'training', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — Military Training', 'Chapter 6. MOS 31E Corrections/Detention (C/D) Specialist', 'MP Investigator, Special Reaction Team, Combatives LV 1, Combatives LV 2, Common Faculty Development-Instructor Course (CFD-IC), and Non-Lethal Weapons Instructor.', '936421dee3d8dccf0b06d0deebc2536635b3b88badeb6fd625cead20bff97919', 18, 18, null, 94, null, null, false, null),
  ('6be32121-d674-536e-bd41-2af7c62652e2', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31E', null, 'assignments', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — Critical Development', 'Chapter 6. MOS 31E Corrections/Detention (C/D) Specialist', 'C/D NCO.', 'f381d9e3b74bcfe7d8e2b5b75586da362c68f445f654cdd434441c0931485b9c', 18, 18, null, 95, null, null, false, null),
  ('e0aa49bc-ef7e-5181-bf25-43ca9141652b', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31E', null, 'assignments', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — Broadening Opportunities', 'Chapter 6. MOS 31E Corrections/Detention (C/D) Specialist', 'MP Investigator, and Housing Unit NCO. Drill Sergeant may fulfill the NCO’s requirement for a key leadership position if the NCO has not had an opportunity to serve in the capacity of Team Leader in the operating force.', '02e5f1ba5d3ef49da2b11be2998edc28ab5a157a5a80a5ac5c5207ddb32086ae', 18, 18, null, 96, null, null, false, null),
  ('86fedc52-119a-5bbc-a6a5-acf644c7d115', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31E', null, 'training', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Institutional Training', 'Chapter 6. MOS 31E Corrections/Detention (C/D) Specialist', 'ALC and EJPME I.', '3dbc8a7581cb58313c988f42490e36e0e906dcb58b4555fceba805e10edc0f81', 19, 19, null, 97, null, null, false, null),
  ('28c6722c-59bd-5337-9748-8b40a75e2f60', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31E', null, 'assignments', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Operational assignments', 'Chapter 6. MOS 31E Corrections/Detention (C/D) Specialist', 'The focus during this phase of their career must be on the continued development and refinement of their leadership skills and broadening their tactical and technical knowledge while serving in operational and corrections assignments. The Key Leadership Position for a Staff Sergeant is Squad Leader. Individual performance and demonstrated potential as a Squad leader may be the deciding factor when it comes to identifying the most qualified Staff Sergeant. A Staff Sergeant with 18 months of key leadership time may be viewed as qualified, 24 months of key leadership time may be viewed as highly qualified, and 30 months or more of key leadership time may be viewed as most qualified. Drill Sergeant may fulfill the NCO’s requirement for a key leadership position if the NCO has not had an opportunity to serve in the capacity of Squad Leader in the operating force. Staff Sergeants serving in a key leadership position higher than their current rank or position, even if it is nonconsecutive with promotion to the next higher rank and that time has been documented by a DA Form 2166-9-2 will count toward key leadership time as a Platoon Sergeant. Without a successful tour of key leadership time as a Squad Leader, a Staff Sergeant will not be competitive for promotion to Sergeant First Class. NCOs should continuously seek opportunities that apply their experience and leadership skills.', '864536841e2495b2550601a762807fac4aa695e726248a90705b6e4e91e89904', 19, 19, null, 98, null, null, false, null),
  ('996657f8-7690-5632-845f-958efbea9c16', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31E', null, 'self_development', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Self-Development', 'Chapter 6. MOS 31E Corrections/Detention (C/D) Specialist', 'American Corrections Association Certified Corrections Officer and Supervisor Course. Staff Sergeants should be working toward an associate’s degree. A college degree is not required for promotion but can be a deciding factor when it comes to identifying the most qualified Staff Sergeant. Soldiers should seek every opportunity to further develop their level of physical fitness. Soldiers should seek opportunities to separate themselves from their peers by obtaining the Expert Soldier Badge. Soldiers serving as Instructors should strive to earn their Basic Army Instructor Badge (BAIB), Senior Army Instructor Badge (SAIB), and Master Army Instructor Badge (MAIB). For additional information on self-development, refer to chapter 3.', '21ca09810df354f229026f98f6a3f4c3947097d0c3991a1774e292fd89864769', 19, 19, null, 99, null, null, false, null),
  ('8a57ef72-d7f6-582f-908b-07624d5babef', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31E', null, 'training', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Military Training', 'Chapter 6. MOS 31E Corrections/Detention (C/D) Specialist', 'Anti-Terrorism Officer (Basic), Conventional Physical Security/Crime Prevention, Combatives LV 1, Combatives LV 2, Combatives Master Trainer, Common Faculty Development -Instructor Course (CFD-IC), Non-Lethal Weapons Instructor, and Master Driver.', '6e698e1a38043a29393f844b3f6319658497c239bdf4311670c0eaaabb2bf6d7', 19, 19, null, 100, null, null, false, null),
  ('4d554fc9-55d0-5879-a199-71d54154a815', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31E', null, 'assignments', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Critical Development', 'Chapter 6. MOS 31E Corrections/Detention (C/D) Specialist', 'Operations NCO, Special Housing Unit Shift Leader, and Watch Commander.', '95417612fa845f76d0f31a52096daabf5d10aebc443c16d566dcf520e3ee64f3', 19, 19, null, 101, null, null, false, null),
  ('1b8c437d-8723-565a-8acd-1f5521f54ae3', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31E', null, 'assignments', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Broadening Opportunities', 'Chapter 6. MOS 31E Corrections/Detention (C/D) Specialist', 'A significant portion of 31E Corrections/Detention Staff Sergeants serve in nominative positions that require a vigorous selection process such as Drill Sergeant. These NCOs are selected based on their above-average performance records, aptitude scores, and demonstrated high legal, moral, and ethical standards. Those who continue to perform at an exceptional level in these assignments should be looked upon favorably for promotion potential. Drill Sergeant Leaders are selected from the very best to serve in positions vital to the Army. Those who complete Drill Sergeant Leader duty are highly qualified and should be looked upon favorably for promotion. Instructor, Small Group Leader, Observer Coach/Trainer, Corrections Supervisor, Corrections Liaison, MP Investigator Supervisor, Housing Unit NCOIC, ACA Plans and Policy NCO, and Project Supervisor.', 'e618eb36a4c694659b965555b21cb79a891b02c6533f3980eea7cee18d0d8702', 19, 19, null, 102, null, null, false, null),
  ('7ce8feb0-6390-5935-9b34-9c420ff1e081', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31E', null, 'training', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Institutional Training', 'Chapter 6. MOS 31E Corrections/Detention (C/D) Specialist', 'SLC and EJPME II.', 'b88a66ba4d2597a58f1225794a558c5305a65a18a25c95187b90151cde77e186', 20, 20, null, 103, null, null, false, null),
  ('70b53c3a-a637-5593-a77e-ba4611c45748', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31E', null, 'assignments', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Operational Assignments', 'Chapter 6. MOS 31E Corrections/Detention (C/D) Specialist', 'At this point in their career, Sergeants First Class should strive for tactical and technical expertise in operational units and corrections assignments. The Key Leadership Position for a Sergeant First Class is Platoon Sergeant. Individual Performance and demonstrated potential as a Platoon Sergeant may be the deciding factor when it comes to identifying the most qualified Sergeant First Class, a Sergeant First Class with 18 months of key leadership time may be viewed as qualified, 24 months of key leadership time may be viewed as highly qualified, and 30 months or more of key leadership time may be viewed as most qualified. Without a successful tour as a Platoon Sergeant, a Sergeant First Class will not be competitive for promotion to Master Sergeant. After serving as a Platoon Sergeant, Sergeants First Class should seek additional time as a Platoon Sergeant or in a broadening assignment. Sergeants First Class serving in a key leadership position higher than their current rank or position, even if it is nonconsecutive with promotion to the next higher rank as long as that time has been documented by a DA Form 2166-9-2 will count toward key leadership time as a First Sergeant. NCOs should continuously seek opportunities that develop and refine their experience and leadership skills.', 'ce38028a9f86f4fd79041828322664606d7526c0ce9541d251aeabc66ec4b3e2', 20, 20, null, 104, null, null, false, null),
  ('72596949-d2b1-50bf-8139-11d6f81e23ca', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31E', null, 'self_development', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Self-Development', 'Chapter 6. MOS 31E Corrections/Detention (C/D) Specialist', 'American Corrections Association Certified Corrections Manager Course. Sergeants First Class that used tuition assistance to the max extent, could possess an associate’s degree and be working toward a bachelor’s degree. A college degree is not required for promotion but can be a deciding factor when it comes to identifying the most qualified Sergeant First Class. Soldiers should seek every opportunity to further develop their level of physical fitness. Soldiers should seek opportunities to separate themselves from their peers by obtaining the Expert Soldier Badge. Soldiers serving as Instructors should strive to earn their Basic Army Instructor Badge (BAIB), Senior Army Instructor Badge (SAIB), and Master Army Instructor Badge (MAIB). For additional information on self-development, refer to chapter 3.', '58f0df1a4735d80ef9e40478a231fcd2bd0d830d12b5f406906900e01afe8b97', 20, 20, null, 105, null, null, false, null),
  ('d815fd08-5736-5355-90f2-271b8d2924c8', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31E', null, 'training', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Military Training', 'Chapter 6. MOS 31E Corrections/Detention (C/D) Specialist', 'Battle Staff NCO, Conventional Physical Security/Crime Prevention, Anti-terrorism Officer (Advanced), Combatives Master Trainer, Common Faculty Development - Instructor Course (CFD-IC), Non-Lethal Weapons Instructor, and Master Driver.', '404fddfb781f02c5a99d7268e402dcd9e914b4c3a75e5a218815fdb4a6cb637b', 20, 20, null, 106, null, null, false, null),
  ('6c527fc1-1ca1-52a6-b903-a2fe769df5d7', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31E', null, 'assignments', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Critical Development', 'Chapter 6. MOS 31E Corrections/Detention (C/D) Specialist', 'A Sergeant First Class should strive to complete an aggregate of 24 months in Critical Development assignments before they consider assignments not directly related to CMF 31. The following assignments are Critical Development assignments: Watch Commander, Operations NCO, and Special Housing Unit NCOIC.', '1127f559bcc914f5492cd6a0a4e526af6eb158431c5de9420b523a0d8fade8f3', 20, 20, null, 107, null, null, false, null),
  ('d68f5793-2ba5-53bc-9194-ff5b8b19f4d0', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31E', null, 'assignments', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Broadening Opportunities', 'Chapter 6. MOS 31E Corrections/Detention (C/D) Specialist', 'A portion of 31E Corrections/Detention Specialist Sergeants First Class serve as Senior Drill Sergeants, a position that requires a rigorous selection process. These NCOs are selected based on their above-average performance records, aptitude scores, and demonstrated high legal, moral, and ethical standards. Those who continue to perform at an exceptional level in these assignments should be looked upon favorably for promotion potential. Those who complete Drill Sergeant Leader duty are highly qualified and should be looked upon favorably for promotion. Instructor, Small Group Leader, Observer Coach/Trainer, Talent Management NCO, Training Developer, System Development NCO, Prisoner Services/Admin NCO, Corrections Supervisor and Plans NCO.', 'bb2075dc7389593abf4a44f3ad26f6e401544c4b1f90f8e8a669174aca5a1893', 20, 20, null, 108, null, null, false, null),
  ('5cbf2ecb-4721-5b2e-b6c5-d92340d9c021', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31E', null, 'training', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/First Sergeant — Institutional Training', 'Chapter 6. MOS 31E Corrections/Detention (C/D) Specialist', 'MLC, SMC, and EJPME II.', 'fe0089d131112e214cf6a8c85d01ec7f9daaf30630662db82641557af093d373', 20, 20, null, 109, null, null, false, null),
  ('c49492cf-060b-5ecd-9fbc-391352ff48bf', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31E', null, 'assignments', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/First Sergeant — Operational Assignments', 'Chapter 6. MOS 31E Corrections/Detention (C/D) Specialist', 'At this level, Master Sergeants should demonstrate tactical and technical mastery of the required knowledge, skills, and behaviors in both operational and corrections assignments. The Key Leadership Position for a Master Sergeant is First Sergeant. Individual Performance and demonstrated potential as a First Sergeant may be the deciding factor when it comes to identifying the most qualified Master Sergeant. A Master Sergeant with 18 months of key leadership time may be viewed as qualified, 24 months of key leadership time may be viewed as highly qualified, and 30 months or more of key leadership time may be viewed as most qualified. After serving as a First Sergeant, Master Sergeants should seek additional First Sergeant time by serving as the First Sergeant of a Headquarters Company or in a broadening assignment related to CMF 31. Master Sergeants serving in a key leadership position higher than their current rank or position, even if it is nonconsecutive with promotion to the next higher rank and that time has been documented by a DA Form 2166-9-3 will count toward key leadership time as an Operations Sergeant Major or Command Sergeant Major. NCOs should continuously seek opportunities that develop and refine their experience and leadership skills.', '9d9b46439209160524a52924a9e976cf5a4a8d2dc844914fb23c8582e70d9d6b', 21, 21, null, 110, null, null, false, null),
  ('95c3b2fb-aaa4-570d-93ab-3cf404b70cf9', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31E', null, 'self_development', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/First Sergeant — Self-Development', 'Chapter 6. MOS 31E Corrections/Detention (C/D) Specialist', 'American Corrections Association Certified Corrections Manager Course. Master Sergeants that used tuition assistance to the max extent, could possess an associate’s degree and be working toward a bachelor’s or graduate degree. A college degree is not required for promotion but can be a deciding factor when it comes to identifying the most qualified Master Sergeant. Soldiers should seek every opportunity to further develop their level of physical fitness. Soldiers should seek opportunities to separate themselves from their peers by obtaining the Expert Soldier Badge. Soldiers serving as Instructors should strive to earn their Basic Army Instructor Badge (BAIB), Senior Army Instructor Badge (SAIB), and Master Army Instructor Badge (MAIB). For additional information refer to chapter 3.', '75e00b1a025dbc0311fec0713282c5f8a9b05e0a86f10079373ea89efa5a7d96', 21, 21, null, 111, null, null, false, null),
  ('56da24cb-1f23-5839-919c-339acdbcf95f', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31E', null, 'training', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/First Sergeant — Military Training', 'Chapter 6. MOS 31E Corrections/Detention (C/D) Specialist', 'Law Enforcement Senior Leaders Course, Battle Staff NCO, Protection Integration Course, Combatives Master Trainer, Common Faculty Development - Instructor Course (CFD-IC), Non-Lethal Weapons Instructor, and Master Driver.', '78dc8af3bfa64b435d947aa6e56e288c038d6ced928e01e17e7d5cb8e437c10f', 21, 21, null, 112, null, null, false, null),
  ('bf6e91f0-7feb-5b6c-a544-03a9c83009e0', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31E', null, 'assignments', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/First Sergeant — Critical Development', 'Chapter 6. MOS 31E Corrections/Detention (C/D) Specialist', 'A Master Sergeant should strive to complete an aggregate of 24 months in Key Development assignments before they consider assignments not directly related to CMF 31. The following assignments are Critical Development assignments. Watch Commander, ACC Ops/Training NCO.', 'd0153cabd5da9f01fbc37c79f37a1c1f775a354c8aececddc3e940a31e145c68', 21, 21, null, 113, null, null, false, null),
  ('127015ba-7c07-587c-a495-3e88c740b27f', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31E', null, 'assignments', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/First Sergeant — Broadening Opportunities', 'Chapter 6. MOS 31E Corrections/Detention (C/D) Specialist', 'Senior Career Management NCO, Inspector General NCO, Training with Industry, and BN/BDE Operations.', 'd9fca09a3ea3647650a5ab85ce55a6d431ec8ceb11c6a34c9f14b0e9bf3694d6', 21, 21, null, 114, null, null, false, null),
  ('5fd8e1a4-e345-5ea2-adef-3348fab22372', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31K', null, 'development', null, null, 'Goals for development', 'Chapter 10. MOS 31K Military Working Dog Handler', 'Soldiers should develop a thorough understanding of the principles of training and handling Military Working Dogs, recognize the three types of learning in the training of Military Working Dogs (habituation, classical conditioning, and instrumental conditioning), and understand the primary and secondary drives in training. They must develop an understanding of how a MWD perceives its environment using its senses during odor detection. MWD Handlers should develop a thorough understanding of MWD capabilities and limitations along with interpersonal communication skills to effectively brief senior leaders. Handlers should continue to develop decoying skills, military and civilian education, and duties with increased responsibilities directly related to MOS 31K.', '73069875e6ca3ae33c3ed910c715bfdee71f344e55402edaef79a4d2ab416455', 22, 22, null, 115, null, null, false, null),
  ('1b7a3fc3-2c57-58e9-be37-6e0e1082b85a', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31K', null, 'training', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private-Specialist/Corporal — Institutional Training', 'Chapter 10. MOS 31K Military Working Dog Handler', 'Formal training (completion of MOS 31K course conducted under the auspices of the USAMPS) is mandatory.', 'e31d6a1bda5a1f6e067613565cf6e1b3b1ab02aa663c9d4465977d86908b5be5', 22, 22, null, 116, null, null, false, null),
  ('6eb26ff0-6217-5a42-9a97-fb0e2c0b9a73', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31K', null, 'assignments', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private-Specialist/Corporal — Operational Assignments', 'Chapter 10. MOS 31K Military Working Dog Handler', 'The key developmental assignment is in a MWD Detachment as a Military Working Dog Handler. Soldiers should develop and seek responsibility and take advantage of opportunities to display their motivation, initiative, and leadership skills in everyday kennel operations.', '08b875e6be8e9671433a464129650cab349a46a5ada8bc46fc831afceb7f8649', 22, 22, null, 117, null, null, false, null),
  ('f5ee937f-9b1e-55ee-84f6-107b7e4e27d0', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31K', null, 'self_development', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private-Specialist/Corporal — Self-Development', 'Chapter 10. MOS 31K Military Working Dog Handler', 'Skill level I Soldiers should develop and seek every opportunity to complete core undergraduate college courses. Soldiers should seek every opportunity to further develop their level of physical fitness. Soldiers should seek opportunities to separate themselves from their peers by obtaining the Expert Soldier Badge. For additional information on self-development, refer to chapter 3.', '428b129eda2dd6c31aa3a6096a23fcadba05d530564a565f4f08715094e48dc0', 22, 22, null, 118, null, null, false, null),
  ('780df253-77b7-5c2d-bf29-780fe2750044', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31K', null, 'training', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private-Specialist/Corporal — Military Training', 'Chapter 10. MOS 31K Military Working Dog Handler', 'Hazardous Materials Courses (Driving and Handling) and Combatives LV 1, Combatives LV 2, Domestic Violence Intervention Training (DVIT), and Child Abuse Prevention Investigative Techniques (CAPIT).', '2913b21e84864b7ed6c5ad717673a0265e1eb664f85a5e6fbeed9c5471625628', 22, 22, null, 119, null, null, false, null),
  ('3f47ddd3-b419-5e40-b5c6-b2a754d0b19d', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31K', null, 'assignments', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private-Specialist/Corporal — Critical Development', 'Chapter 10. MOS 31K Military Working Dog Handler', 'N/A', 'e2f79e5b60330bba4c289962231b6ba2957d0b14e7deb3110417003c79dea635', 22, 22, null, 120, null, null, false, null),
  ('a871f6ad-86c7-5f47-b013-6733951e9959', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31K', null, 'assignments', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private-Specialist/Corporal — Broadening Opportunities', 'Chapter 10. MOS 31K Military Working Dog Handler', 'Assignment as an Instructor (Military Working Dog Trainer) at the Military Working Dog Course at Joint Base San Antonio-Lackland. Soldiers graduating the MWD Handlers Course and directly assigned to D/701st should not exceed 24 months on station at Joint Base San Antonio-Lackland. Soldiers should not be assigned to D/701st more than one time at this rank.', 'f989feecd7e858a07330e864a0c4ed592f32075692cc2b1545c69b2e1cb6b3ed', 22, 22, null, 121, null, null, false, null),
  ('55b3780b-a717-575e-b9c0-cf77205b7f00', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31K', null, 'training', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — Institutional Training', 'Chapter 10. MOS 31K Military Working Dog Handler', 'BLC and EJPME I.', 'ddfec96d62e4c8b81dfe220560dcf5e578ab6d5a83acf74a5a88662e4f9dda44', 23, 23, null, 122, null, null, false, null),
  ('b6518cde-3966-5b04-83d2-f4f96d6d2edb', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31K', null, 'assignments', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — Operational Assignments', 'Chapter 10. MOS 31K Military Working Dog Handler', 'The focus during this phase of their career should be on developing leadership skills and tactical and technical expertise and knowledge in both operation and law enforcement assignments. The Key Leadership Position for a Sergeant is Team Leader. A Sergeant with 18 months of key leadership time may be viewed as qualified, 24 months of key leadership time may be viewed as highly qualified, and 30 months or more of key leadership time may be viewed as most qualified. Sergeants serving in a key leadership position higher than their current rank or position, even if it is nonconsecutive with promotion to the next higher rank and that time has been documented by a DA Form 2166-9-1 will count toward key leadership time as a Squad Leader. NCOs should continuously seek opportunities that develop and refine their experience and leadership skills.', '012bae9eaf4d7f8faf31c8d3f2861a2e4d0241f8b6ff31ffff0808b62da3c552', 23, 23, null, 123, null, null, false, null),
  ('2956d18c-cc72-5853-97dc-9f02f8cc536b', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31K', null, 'self_development', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — Self-Development', 'Chapter 10. MOS 31K Military Working Dog Handler', 'Sergeants are encouraged to work toward an associate’s degree. Soldiers should seek every opportunity to further develop their level of physical fitness. Soldiers should seek opportunities to separate themselves from their peers by obtaining the Expert Soldier Badge. Soldiers serving as Instructors should strive to earn their Basic Army Instructor Badge (BAIB), Senior Army Instructor Badge (SAIB), and Master Army Instructor Badge (MAIB). For additional information on self-development, refer to chapter 3.', '86bafa5b55a4a665312fd10934ccb42b869301b06d8166e1b5df25a08cd6e9c8', 23, 23, null, 124, null, null, false, null),
  ('73fa82fb-5643-5c15-a4e2-19e390ae3e83', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31K', null, 'training', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — Military Training', 'Chapter 10. MOS 31K Military Working Dog Handler', 'Technical Transportation of HAZMAT, Basic Instructors Course (USAF), Combatives LV 1, Combatives LV 2, Combatives Master Trainer, Domestic Violence Intervention Training (DVIT), and Child Abuse Prevention Investigative Techniques (CAPIT), Common Faculty Development -Instructor Course (CFD-IC), and Non-Lethal Weapons Instructor.', '37b7734097416e5c32a366f9748bdcfb499ccc38a156c16e435f08fcb1b5e83e', 23, 23, null, 125, null, null, false, null),
  ('a725c3b7-123d-54ec-bc6a-44ad3d3cc69f', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31K', null, 'assignments', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — Critical Development', 'Chapter 10. MOS 31K Military Working Dog Handler', 'N/A.', '190a648f47ed454cf70ff2bd90dc842faae3f1fe19810cedf4bda2fbedb8201e', 23, 23, null, 126, null, null, false, null),
  ('8ea604aa-cb6d-5f55-8e9c-43c5e8e154f5', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31K', null, 'assignments', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — Broadening Opportunities', 'Chapter 10. MOS 31K Military Working Dog Handler', 'Assignment as an Instructor (Military Working Dog Trainer) at the Military Working Dog Course at Joint Base San Antonio-Lackland. Soldiers should not be assigned to D/701st more than one time at this rank.', 'ec9b153da13ff3f87cd2052ccae0fb9cb53dd072f6029fc043d393d6e3943639', 23, 23, null, 127, null, null, false, null),
  ('1aa2c48f-bf14-5917-aa8a-94560193e2ee', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31K', null, 'training', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Institutional Training', 'Chapter 10. MOS 31K Military Working Dog Handler', 'ALC and EJPME I.', '3dbc8a7581cb58313c988f42490e36e0e906dcb58b4555fceba805e10edc0f81', 23, 23, null, 128, null, null, false, null),
  ('79fe69b0-c24e-5d6d-a4c6-ef263e5ec5a4', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31K', null, 'assignments', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Operational Assignments', 'Chapter 10. MOS 31K Military Working Dog Handler', 'The focus during this phase of their career should be on developing leadership skills and tactical and technical expertise and knowledge in both operation and law enforcement assignments. The Key Leadership Position for a Sergeant is Squad Leader. A Staff Sergeant with 18 months of key leadership time may be viewed as qualified, 24 months of key leadership time may be viewed as highly qualified, and 30 months or more of key leadership time may be viewed as most qualified. Staff Sergeants serving in a key leadership position higher than their current rank or position, even if it is nonconsecutive with promotion to the next higher rank and that time has been documented by a DA Form 2166-9-2 will count toward key leadership time as a Kennel Master. NCOs should continuously seek opportunities that develop and refine their experience and leadership skills. Without a successful tour of key leadership time as a Squad Leader, a Staff Sergeant will not be competitive for promotion to Sergeant First Class. NCOs should continuously seek opportunities that apply their experience and leadership skills.', '3d8ef9430cf8e998f2f33cca3dc2cb06d89636e1cf0ef732492d3e4411190ef4', 23, 23, null, 129, null, null, false, null),
  ('cb6e7fe2-3f07-5439-be0c-44eadcd96416', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31K', null, 'self_development', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Self-Development', 'Chapter 10. MOS 31K Military Working Dog Handler', 'Staff Sergeants should be working towards an associate’s degree. A college degree is not required for promotion but can be a deciding factor when it comes to identifying the most qualified Staff Sergeant. Soldiers should seek every opportunity to further develop their level of physical fitness. Soldiers should seek opportunities to separate themselves from their peers by obtaining the Expert Soldier Badge. Soldiers serving as Instructors should strive to earn their Basic Army Instructor Badge (BAIB), Senior Army Instructor Badge (SAIB), and Master Army Instructor Badge (MAIB). For additional information on self-development, refer to chapter 3.', '76e81c819ea28514a2f42a9705b6cb9f11b60504c6856ccfdd586a1221aff0ac', 24, 24, null, 130, null, null, false, null),
  ('d8390623-b7ba-50b1-a3c5-99d44427feb2', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31K', null, 'training', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Military Training', 'Chapter 10. MOS 31K Military Working Dog Handler', 'Military Working Dog Trainer/Kennel Master Course, Anti-Terrorism Officer (Basic), Conventional Physical Security/Crime Prevention, Combatives LV 2, Combatives Master Trainer, Common Faculty Development -Instructor Course (CFD-IC), Non-Lethal Weapons Instructor, and Master Driver.', '29d84ac33b7214c342e8e5cd71c2fc59589da2920b7b82542b0f3f3bca5d6346', 24, 24, null, 131, null, null, false, null),
  ('735cf4c5-8a05-53ed-a5aa-e3e30839a6bb', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31K', null, 'assignments', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Critical Development', 'Chapter 10. MOS 31K Military Working Dog Handler', 'MWD Plans NCO and Operations NCO. Consideration for promotion to Sergeant First Class is considerably weighed for those Staff Sergeants having served at least 12 months as a Military Working Dog Plans NCO post-Key Leadership Position.', '3baa2a4c234bb4a561b0af3de64a4bbe8236cfdf8691057722ff404668c5ccf6', 24, 24, null, 132, null, null, false, null),
  ('ee70c50e-1a0c-5602-8686-51d4991db649', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31K', null, 'assignments', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Broadening Opportunities', 'Chapter 10. MOS 31K Military Working Dog Handler', 'Drill Sergeant may fulfill the NCO’s requirement for a key leadership position if the NCO has not had an opportunity to serve in the capacity of Squad Leader in the operating force. Instructor, Small Group Leader, MWD Handler O/C, and MWD Trainer. Soldiers should not be assigned to C/701st or D/701st more than one time at this rank.', 'f41a9ad14a56d6e77350312e15c85bcf53beb7e00ede0f9acccc69fd5d496006', 24, 24, null, 133, null, null, false, null),
  ('a956fc3c-9f94-5457-bf6d-f013eedea4a2', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31K', null, 'training', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Institutional Training', 'Chapter 10. MOS 31K Military Working Dog Handler', 'SLC and EJPME II.', 'b88a66ba4d2597a58f1225794a558c5305a65a18a25c95187b90151cde77e186', 24, 24, null, 134, null, null, false, null),
  ('aeca1a36-7960-5e94-8e29-0e0a06886083', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31K', null, 'assignments', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Operational assignments', 'Chapter 10. MOS 31K Military Working Dog Handler', 'At this point in their career, Sergeants First Class should strive for tactical and technical expertise in operational units and law enforcement assignments. The Key Leadership Position for a Sergeant First Class is Kennel Master. Individual Performance and demonstrated potential as a Kennel Master may be the deciding factor when it comes to identifying the most qualified Sergeant First Class, a Sergeant First Class with 18 months of key leadership time may be viewed as qualified, 24 months of key leadership time may be viewed as highly qualified, and 30 months or more of key leadership time may be viewed as most qualified. Without a successful tour as a Kennel Master, a Sergeant First Class will not be competitive for promotion to Master Sergeant. After serving as a Kennel Master, Sergeants First Class should seek additional time as a Kennel Master or in a broadening assignment. Sergeants First Class serving in a key leadership position higher than their current rank or position, even if it is nonconsecutive with promotion to the next higher rank and that time has been documented by a DA Form 2166-9-2 will count toward key leadership time as a First Sergeant or MWD Program Manager. NCOs should continuously seek opportunities that apply their experience and leadership skills.', 'cc985ea6215abb3c296ae70d5c73ac55816bf32b9c5ad15ac65f3112a584b700', 24, 24, null, 135, null, null, false, null),
  ('732d3ae8-7132-570e-82c0-a8796b104e74', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31K', null, 'self_development', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Self-Development', 'Chapter 10. MOS 31K Military Working Dog Handler', 'Sergeants First Class that used tuition assistance to the max extent, could possess an associate’s degree and be working toward a bachelor’s degree. A college degree is not required for promotion but can be a deciding factor when it comes to identifying the most qualified Sergeant First Class. Soldiers should seek every opportunity to further develop their level of physical fitness. Soldiers should seek opportunities to separate themselves from their peers by obtaining the Expert Soldier Badge. Soldiers serving as Instructors should strive to earn their Basic Army Instructor Badge (BAIB), Senior Army Instructor Badge (SAIB), and Master Army Instructor Badge (MAIB). For additional information on self-development, refer to chapter 3.', '4d9aa7335e59256f90d5c03b9e9c90d138e41b65030fca71a2a547f73a731cc9', 24, 25, null, 136, null, null, false, null),
  ('b73b5432-18a3-5db6-8aa4-95f49d90d21f', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31K', null, 'training', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Military Training', 'Chapter 10. MOS 31K Military Working Dog Handler', 'Military Working Dog Trainer/Kennel Master Course, Conventional Physical Security/Crime Prevention, Anti-Terrorism Officer (Basic), and Battle Staff NCO, Protection Integration Course, Law Enforcement Senior Leaders Course, Combatives Master Trainer, Common Faculty Development -Instructor Course (CFD-IC), Non-Lethal Weapons Instructor, and Master Driver.', '3a4c1d89ab40c035a13243f95bc35a66ea1476a955dd834fb31a40d4ae77f635', 25, 25, null, 137, null, null, false, null),
  ('26edf9ef-e214-5e89-a53f-a041b6cb5df6', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31K', null, 'assignments', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Critical Development', 'Chapter 10. MOS 31K Military Working Dog Handler', 'Operations NCO and BDE MWD Operations NCO.', '904ddf458d6bbf38eec856244bc6a77ba3f641fad9984f5178a45f619d4837cf', 25, 25, null, 138, null, null, false, null),
  ('bd142fb6-abba-5dcb-b9b1-c9a85c0ba43f', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31K', null, 'assignments', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Broadening Opportunities', 'Chapter 10. MOS 31K Military Working Dog Handler', 'Drill Sergeant may fulfill the NCO’s requirement for a key leadership position if the NCO has not had an opportunity to serve in the capacity of Kennel Master in the operating force. Small Group Leader, Instructor, MWD Trainer, Talent Management NCO (HRC), Multi-Purpose Canine Program Manager, and Senior Training Management NCO (MWD); while career-enhancing, generally does not allow for the same level of leadership development and should not be considered appropriate alternatives to key leadership positions.', '74bc14897a0b159eab0b1fff607edf6e7eb4fe7776b0df51fa018f5a449a0515', 25, 25, null, 139, null, null, false, null),
  ('89ab11ea-2118-518b-a9b6-5e2ad0841e34', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31K', null, 'training', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/First Sergeant — Institutional Training', 'Chapter 10. MOS 31K Military Working Dog Handler', 'MLC, SMC, and EJPME II.', 'fe0089d131112e214cf6a8c85d01ec7f9daaf30630662db82641557af093d373', 25, 25, null, 140, null, null, false, null),
  ('fecb176e-9451-5237-b52c-78fc92584339', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31K', null, 'assignments', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/First Sergeant — Operational Assignments', 'Chapter 10. MOS 31K Military Working Dog Handler', 'Operational Assignments At this level, Master Sergeants are required to demonstrate tactical and technical mastery of the required knowledge, skill, and behaviors in both operational and law enforcement assignments. The Key Leadership Position for a Master Sergeant is First Sergeant or MWD Program Manager. Individual Performance and demonstrated potential as a First Sergeant or MWD Program Manager may be the deciding factor when it comes to identifying the most qualified Master Sergeant, a Master Sergeant with 18 months of key leadership time may be viewed as qualified, 24 months of key leadership time may be viewed as highly qualified, and 30 months or more of key leadership time may be viewed as most qualified. After serving as a First Sergeant or MWD Program manager, Master Sergeants should seek additional First Sergeant or MWD Program manager; or in a broadening assignment. Master Sergeants serving in a key leadership position higher than their current rank or position, even if it is nonconsecutive with promotion to the next higher rank and that time has been documented by a DA Form 2166-9-3 will count toward key leadership time as an Operations Sergeant Major or Command Sergeant Major. NCOs should continuously seek opportunities that apply their experience and leadership skills.', 'd6f4f3b16d1fc02f29b0deaebbfa5488875a4e87b275c9159dc65ed2e3d819e3', 25, 25, null, 141, null, null, false, null),
  ('7aef879f-01fe-5f4c-be9e-9a96d3bf0c11', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31K', null, 'self_development', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/First Sergeant — Self-Development', 'Chapter 10. MOS 31K Military Working Dog Handler', 'Master Sergeants should possess an associate’s degree and be working toward a bachelor’s degree. Soldiers should seek every opportunity to further develop their level of physical fitness. Soldiers should seek opportunities to separate themselves from their peers by obtaining the Expert Soldier Badge. Soldiers serving as Instructors should strive to earn their Basic Army Instructor Badge (BAIB), Senior Army Instructor Badge (SAIB), and Master Army Instructor Badge (MAIB). For additional information on self-development, refer to chapter 3.', 'b75c497369383fad44cd807fc77587af94414e42994f6dbfab24d1b7a9626396', 25, 25, null, 142, null, null, false, null),
  ('be7a02b0-83de-5eed-9fda-ff0331d4ed46', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31K', null, 'training', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/First Sergeant — Military Training', 'Chapter 10. MOS 31K Military Working Dog Handler', 'Law Enforcement Senior Leaders Course, Capabilities Development Course, Battle Staff NCO, and Protection Integration Course.', '4b0bb4e4ea84f4d0305767c5089b7bc751d57d440dad8c370b7ef2e705df63d6', 25, 25, null, 143, null, null, false, null),
  ('d31bb90b-455b-5f8c-87dc-84141bcdfa2a', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31K', null, 'assignments', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/First Sergeant — Critical Development', 'Chapter 10. MOS 31K Military Working Dog Handler', 'A Master Sergeant should strive to complete an aggregate of 24 months in Key Development assignments before they consider assignments not directly related to CMF 31. The following assignments are Key Development assignments. MWD Course Manager.', 'e1707d9ec31b8337747d6672ace47f4e5a2d0540d46e144fe1542dd56f236178', 25, 26, null, 144, null, null, false, null),
  ('2ee959b8-f5d6-51b0-8c5e-149ae5559c14', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31K', null, 'assignments', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/First Sergeant — Broadening Opportunities', 'Chapter 10. MOS 31K Military Working Dog Handler', 'MWD Career Management NCO, Senior Training Developer, and Training Development Systems NCO (MWD).', 'c5f36ced69403524365fb75920e9863789a16a89d6f6b941915a667fc4d35e71', 26, 26, null, 145, null, null, false, null),
  ('2bca85df-2d57-5d80-bb6d-540cc3b338a6', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31Z', null, 'development', null, null, 'Goals for development', 'Chapter 12. MOS 31Z Senior Military Police Sergeant, Sergeant Major / Command Sergeant Major', 'This is the culmination for all MOSs in CMF31. The career development plans preceding this MOS will assist Soldiers in arriving at this level. MOS 31Z, Senior NCOs will fill positions in both TOE and TDA organizations. Individual Performance and demonstrated potential at each level of the SGM/CSM Professional Development Proficiency Code table may be the deciding factor when it comes to identifying the most qualified Sergeant Major/Command Sergeant Major to be selected on the Battalion/Brigade Centralized Section List.', '927e5518a559814e6407fa4c21f5a9c7cda9a4c2b11f63872313bf9e7d7bad35', 26, 27, null, 146, null, null, false, null),
  ('e3b7ccc9-addb-59dc-a95e-3499eec4c91a', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31Z', null, 'training', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major/Command Sergeant Major — Institutional Training', 'Chapter 12. MOS 31Z Senior Military Police Sergeant, Sergeant Major / Command Sergeant Major', 'All Sergeants Major must be graduates of the US Army Sergeants Major Academy (USASMA) and EJPME II. Sergeants Major and Command Sergeants Major may attend a variety of training based on the requirements of their duty positions.', '704be465f1aa507e4fc8216280eb67c3c44f3442ed759516ce3b3d6a2340e1cf', 27, 27, null, 147, null, null, false, null),
  ('7184db16-d7ec-580a-bbb0-e73ff001c874', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31Z', null, 'assignments', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major/Command Sergeant Major — Operational Assignments', 'Chapter 12. MOS 31Z Senior Military Police Sergeant, Sergeant Major / Command Sergeant Major', 'The Key Leadership position for Sergeant Major is Battalion or Brigade/Group Command Sergeant Major for a minimum of 24 months at each level.', 'be29a4abda5b20406fe9712405649cceff787ec8cd32738c427ee94c52c2d93a', 27, 27, null, 148, null, null, false, null),
  ('1b7d1384-c229-514d-bf66-bbd7a2e097f1', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31Z', null, 'self_development', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major/Command Sergeant Major — Self-Development', 'Chapter 12. MOS 31Z Senior Military Police Sergeant, Sergeant Major / Command Sergeant Major', 'Sergeants Major that used tuition assistance to the max extent, could hold a bachelor’s degree and be working towards a master’s degree. A college degree is not required for further advancement but can be a deciding factor when it comes to identifying the most qualified Sergeant Major/Command Sergeant Major. Sergeants Major should take advantage of civilian education opportunities and actively seek to acquire the skills required to perform at the highest level, Sergeants Major who fail to develop new and complex skills required to perform in Operational and Generating units may be considered less adaptive and agile than their peers. Experience and leadership skills gained through a variety of challenging and developmental duty assignments are paramount for progression and selection as a CSM or nominative position. For additional information on self-development, refer to chapter 3.', 'aa16e7767c4f396d8e4928f262f56ef9b045f2272adf8b9a4867a770478239d5', 27, 27, null, 149, null, null, false, null),
  ('40f20827-0ef5-5841-8f2a-c141f4eb56ae', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31Z', null, 'training', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major/Command Sergeant Major — Military Training', 'Chapter 12. MOS 31Z Senior Military Police Sergeant, Sergeant Major / Command Sergeant Major', 'Law Enforcement Senior Leaders Course (LESLC), Military Police Pre- Command Course (MPCC), Senior Leaders Seminar, and Keystone Course, Force Management Orientation Course, How the Army Runs Course.', '6cea9524a473df7ec42c6f4d475f1dbfe567e5819fc8c653e2388b7d6ae4c2fa', 27, 27, null, 150, null, null, false, null),
  ('332e26a9-520c-5e87-bd35-70604d8d8718', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31Z', null, 'assignments', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major/Command Sergeant Major — Key Development', 'Chapter 12. MOS 31Z Senior Military Police Sergeant, Sergeant Major / Command Sergeant Major', 'Battalion/Brigade/Group Operations Sergeant Major, Facility SGM, Directorate of Emergency Services SGM, Chief Career Management NCO, G2/G3 Sergeant Major at CID Headquarters, Military District of Washington Provost SGM, and Provost NCO (TRADOC/FORSCOM/IMCOM/USAREUR-AF/USARPAC).', '51f420dfefb64b509af718761e4963624d24e88166471da30c34942b1e56ea9a', 27, 27, null, 151, null, null, false, null),
  ('31c1de38-5739-5e87-8e52-46beaeaa3e02', '3d2243c3-0cfb-575a-af17-16ddd641b250', 'Army', 'enlisted', '31Z', null, 'assignments', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major/Command Sergeant Major — Broadening Opportunities', 'Chapter 12. MOS 31Z Senior Military Police Sergeant, Sergeant Major / Command Sergeant Major', 'Basic Military Police Training Division SGM, Directorate of Training SGM (USAMPS), Directorate of Plans and Operations SGM (USAMPS), NCOA Assistant Commandant (MSCOE), Enlisted MP Branch SGM, USASMA Small Group Leader, USASMA Fellowship, Military Working Dog Program Sergeant Major, and Observer-Coach Command Sergeant Major (2x Bn CSM).', '18ac4f64b6c91563c4ddbd2a275cd051d4ac6780fc310ad6b5423812355a373f', 27, 27, null, 152, null, null, false, null);
